 ## Overview
-IndexGrid is a sparse data structure derived from NanoVDB, designed to aggressively accelerate stencil-centric operations (e.g., convolution) while serving as an effective acceleration structure for ray marching and other spatial operations [S1]. It solves the problem of memory inefficiency and code complexity in standard VDB implementations by separating topology (sparsity pattern) from values (features) [S1]. Unlike hash tables, which lack spatial coherence, or octrees, which suffer from $O(\log n)$ access times, IndexGrid uses a fixed-depth shallow tree to maintain $O(1)$ amortized access while preserving spatial coherence [S1].
+IndexGrid is a sparse data structure derived from NanoVDB, designed to aggressively accelerate stencil-centric operations (e.g., convolution) while serving as an effective acceleration structure for ray marching and other spatial operations [S1]. It solves the problem of memory inefficiency and code complexity in standard VDB implementations by separating topology (sparsity pattern) from values (features) [S1]. Unlike hash tables, which lack spatial coherence, or octrees, which suffer from $O(\log n)$ access times, IndexGrid uses a fixed-depth shallow tree to maintain $O(1)$ amortized access while preserving spatial coherence [S1]. It builds on [Sparse Voxel Chunk Indexing](8606-sparse-voxel-chunk-indexing.md) and differs from [GPU Accelerated Sparse Grid Construction](9812-gpu-accelerated-sparse-grid-construction.md) by prioritizing a fixed-depth shallow VDB tree for ML feature storage rather than dynamic grid construction [S1].
 
